      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "S100B, a plasma marker of brain injury, was compared after coronary artery bypass grafting with and without cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Fourteen patients with off-pump operations and 18 patients with CPB were compared. Seven patients in the off-pump group underwent a minithoracotomy and received only an arterial graft, whereas 7 patients underwent sternotomy and received both an arterial and one or two vein grafts. S100B was measured in arterial plasma using an immunoassay with enhanced sensitivity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "S100B before the operation was 0.03 microg/L. At wound closure, S100B in patients of the off-pump and CPB groups reached a maximum level of 0.22 +/- 0.07 and 2.4 +/- 1.5 microg/L, respectively (p < 0.001). No strokes occurred. Patients without CPB receiving arterial and vein grafts released slightly more S100B (p < 0.05) than patients with only arterial grafting. In patients undergoing CPB, S100B increased slightly before aortic cannulation (p < 0.001), to the same level as the maximum reached for the non-CPB group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Coronary artery bypass grafting with CPB caused a 10-fold greater increase in S100B than off-pump grafting. S100B release after off-pump sternotomy with vein grafting was slightly greater than in arterial grafting through a minithoracotomy."
        }
      ]},
